course: dictionary
lesson: 3813
title: Forever: for all time, or just very long?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Forever is an adverb, and it can mean three different things. The words around it tell you which one. «I'll love you forever» means for all time, with no end. There is no last day.
explain: «The queue took forever» means for a very long time. Nobody is really waiting for all time; it just feels that long. This meaning is informal, so you will hear it in everyday talk more than in serious writing.
explain: «He's forever losing his keys» means again and again, all the time. The clue is that losing his keys happens over and over. Check by swapping in your meaning: does «all the time» still make sense? If it does, you have found the right one.
fact: Forever is an adverb, and in the UK it can also be written as «for ever».
fact: «Forever» can mean for all time, with no end, as in «I'll love you forever».
fact: In everyday talk, «It took forever» means it took a very long time.
fact: «Forever» can also mean again and again, all the time, as in «He's forever losing his keys».
fact: Scientists do not know whether the universe has an edge or goes on forever; many think it has no edge.
exercise: «I'll love you forever.» What does forever mean here? | Only now and then | For a very short moment | Quickly and suddenly | *For all time, with no end
exercise: «The queue took forever.» In this sentence, forever means | Very quickly | Again and again | *For a very long time | For all time, with no end
exercise: Find and copy the word from «He's forever losing his keys» that tells you it happens all the time. | losing | *forever | keys | his
exercise: Sam says «forever» always means for all time. Is Sam right? | No, it never means anything at all | Yes, it always means for all time, with no end | *No, it can also mean a very long time, or again and again | Yes, but only in stories
test: «Scientists do not know whether the universe goes on forever.» What might goes on forever mean? | Is very small | Is made of sand | Stops next Tuesday | *Has no end
test: «The baby is forever giggling.» What does forever mean in this sentence? | For all time, with no end | *All the time, again and again | Very quietly | Only once
exam: Which is the best way to check your meaning of forever in a sentence? | Change the word to a longer one | Give up and skip the whole text | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Look only at the last word of the sentence
